{"name":"Ultramarine Highlight","hex":"#2e328f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ultramarine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Beyond the sea.","Of a brilliant dark blue or slightly purplish color like that of the pigment (noun sense 1)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["In full ultramarine blue: a brilliant blue pigment traditionally made from ground-up lapis lazuli, and now usually either extracted from mineral deposits or made synthetically.","A brilliant dark blue or slightly purplish colour like that of the pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ultramarine"},{"term":"highlight","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An area or a spot in a drawing, painting, or photograph that is strongly illuminated.","An especially significant or interesting detail or event or period of time."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make prominent; emphasize.","To be a highlight of."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/highlight"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ultramarine","blocks":[{"text":"Ultramarine is a deep blue pigment historically derived from lazurite, the principal component of the metamorphic rock lapis lazuli. The pigment was produced by grinding the stone and undergoing a complex washing process to isolate the blue mineral, which made it exceptionally valuable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ultramarinepigment.jpg/330px-Ultramarinepigment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultramarine","wikidata":"Q219660"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/Natural_ultramarine_painted.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/Natural_ultramarine_painted.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Natural ultram